WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:08.435
We begin with an empty Survive Drive trip, using Santa Clarita as the start and Long Beach as the destination.

00:00:08.435 --> 00:00:15.736
Enter the two places in the trip panel, then check that the resolved locations are the ones you intended.

00:00:15.736 --> 00:00:21.499
Similar place names can refer to different locations, so this is a useful first check.

00:00:21.499 --> 00:00:26.495
At this point, we have not searched for routes or requested property evidence.

00:00:26.495 --> 00:00:35.717
Think of the workflow as a sequence: set the trip, find alternatives, compare the available evidence, inspect the finalists, and make your own choice.

00:00:35.717 --> 00:00:39.175
This recording follows that sequence in the actual app.

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:47.141
Before searching, review the road preferences.

00:00:47.141 --> 00:00:52.321
In this example, the highway and toll controls remain at their neutral defaults.

00:00:52.321 --> 00:00:56.705
Those settings affect the alternatives that the routing engine will consider.

00:00:56.705 --> 00:01:01.885
Choose the preferences that fit your trip before you generate the candidate pool.

00:01:01.885 --> 00:01:09.855
A change in preferences can produce a different search, so the routes and their evidence need to be reviewed again.

00:01:09.855 --> 00:01:14.636
Notice that routing and property sampling are separate decisions in this workflow.

00:01:14.636 --> 00:01:19.020
Setting up the road search does not require a property lookup.

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:24.200
We are preparing the alternatives first, then deciding which alternatives need additional evidence.

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:35.714
Escape Gates give the search more ways to leave the starting area and approach the destination.

00:01:35.714 --> 00:01:40.188
Here, I request four suggested gates near each end of the trip.

00:01:40.188 --> 00:01:43.543
The app displays the circles and suggested road locations.

00:01:43.543 --> 00:01:46.898
There is also a no-gate option at each end.

00:01:46.898 --> 00:01:50.625
That gives us five starting choices and five destination choices.

00:01:50.625 --> 00:01:53.235
Five times five is twenty five pairings.

00:01:53.235 --> 00:01:58.081
This is why the search can explore more than a single direct route.

00:01:58.081 --> 00:02:04.418
A gate is part of the route search, not a declaration that the surrounding area is safe.

00:02:04.418 --> 00:02:07.400
Check the suggestions in the map before proceeding.

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:17.858
The local Valhalla routing server searches the twenty five start and destination pairings.

00:02:17.858 --> 00:02:22.573
The completed search returns twenty seven distinct route candidates in this session.

00:02:22.573 --> 00:02:30.432
Pairings and candidates are different counts: a pairing can yield route alternatives, and the resulting pool is what we compare.

00:02:30.432 --> 00:02:33.968
No property lookup was needed to generate these routes.

00:02:33.968 --> 00:02:42.612
We now have a set of possible paths, with distance and travel time information, but generating a candidate does not endorse it.

00:02:42.612 --> 00:02:50.078
The next task is to compare those paths using saved evidence and decide where additional sampling will be useful.

00:02:50.078 --> 00:02:54.400
I have cut the waiting between the request and the results.

00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:04.009
Round Zero starts with evidence already available in the shared cache.

00:03:04.009 --> 00:03:08.655
It ranks the full candidate pool before asking for new property lookups.

00:03:08.655 --> 00:03:12.526
Pay attention to known coverage as well as the ranking.

00:03:12.526 --> 00:03:20.269
In this session, all twenty seven candidates begin below twenty percent known coverage, so the first screen protects them all.

00:03:20.269 --> 00:03:24.528
Included means that a route remains eligible for another evidence round.

00:03:24.528 --> 00:03:28.787
It does not mean that the route is recommended for driving.

00:03:28.787 --> 00:03:33.433
Unknown cells remain unknown; they are not assigned a low property value.

00:03:33.433 --> 00:03:41.950
A strong-looking score based on limited coverage deserves a different level of confidence from a score supported across most of the route.

00:03:47.750 --> 00:03:51.319
Now we check the cost of the first evidence round.

00:03:51.319 --> 00:03:56.316
The per-route quota controls how many new samples we request for an included route.

00:03:56.316 --> 00:04:03.098
Five per route would exceed the current batch limit in this example, so I reduce the quota to three.

00:04:03.098 --> 00:04:08.094
I also change a candidate selection briefly, which lets us see the estimate respond.

00:04:08.094 --> 00:04:12.734
The free cache check identifies twenty six unique cells that still need evidence.

00:04:12.734 --> 00:04:16.660
Shared cells are counted once, even when several routes use them.

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:24.156
At this session's rate of one credit and one cent per new lookup, the plan is capped at twenty six cents.

00:04:24.156 --> 00:04:27.725
Review that exact count before using the separate Run control.

00:04:32.750 --> 00:04:36.484
The first evidence round makes those twenty six RentCast requests.

00:04:36.484 --> 00:04:42.831
Each returned cell can update every route that shares it, so one lookup can improve several comparisons.

00:04:42.831 --> 00:04:45.818
All twenty six calls succeed in this session.

00:04:45.818 --> 00:04:53.658
Once the round completes, the app reranks the full pool of twenty seven candidates, and eleven remain included for further sampling.

00:04:53.658 --> 00:04:58.512
The completed round is the point at which the next cull takes effect.

00:04:58.512 --> 00:05:05.233
Keep looking at the amount of evidence behind each route, rather than reading the new order as certainty.

00:05:05.233 --> 00:05:12.700
The progress wait has been shortened here; the completed count and the resulting pool are the useful checkpoints to read.

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:21.366
The map adds geographic context to the route cards.

00:05:21.366 --> 00:05:27.795
It shows the selected route, colored property cells, and highway segments excluded from the property comparison.

00:05:27.795 --> 00:05:32.616
The colors describe the app's property-value signal; they are not personal-safety ratings.

00:05:32.616 --> 00:05:38.643
Known coverage also matters because a colored patch is only part of the route's evidence.

00:05:38.643 --> 00:05:43.062
Here I inspect a separate cell away from the candidate route.

00:05:43.062 --> 00:05:46.679
The app identifies that cell as outside the route.

00:05:46.679 --> 00:05:51.500
Evidence retrieved for an outside cell does not change the route ranking.

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:57.527
The full demo session includes one separate map lookup in addition to its route rounds.

00:05:57.527 --> 00:06:02.750
Keep route evidence and exploratory map lookups distinct when reviewing the session cost.

00:06:07.750 --> 00:06:10.530
Before another round, compare the route cards.

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:16.091
Distance, travel time, known coverage, and weighted price per square foot answer different questions.

00:06:16.091 --> 00:06:21.651
A route that scores well on one measure can involve a tradeoff on another.

00:06:21.651 --> 00:06:25.225
The next free check identifies fourteen new shared-cell lookups.

00:06:25.225 --> 00:06:28.800
After those fourteen calls finish, seven routes remain included.

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:35.949
The full pool is still reranked: a previously excluded route can return if the evidence changes its position.

00:06:35.949 --> 00:06:39.126
Earlier inclusion does not permanently reserve a slot.

00:06:39.126 --> 00:06:49.850
Use the next round to answer a comparison that still matters, and review the exact lookup count again rather than assuming it will match the previous round.

00:06:55.750 --> 00:06:58.459
The third plan identifies ten new cells.

00:06:58.459 --> 00:07:03.878
I review the plan and use the separate Run control to start the requests.

00:07:03.878 --> 00:07:08.522
Once they finish, the included pool falls from seven routes to four.

00:07:08.522 --> 00:07:16.263
This session uses a thirty five percent culling setting for the evidence rounds, with whole-route rounding shown in the interface.

00:07:16.263 --> 00:07:22.843
The shrinking pool directs new sampling toward fewer alternatives, while the full ranking remains available for comparison.

00:07:22.843 --> 00:07:26.326
It does not remove uncertainty from the property signal.

00:07:26.326 --> 00:07:32.131
Read the completed round status, then inspect which routes moved and how their coverage changed.

00:07:32.131 --> 00:07:37.550
I have removed the idle wait while keeping the plan and the completed result.

00:07:42.750 --> 00:07:45.755
The fourth exact plan uses nine more lookups.

00:07:45.755 --> 00:07:51.015
At completion, two routes remain included and twenty five are excluded from further sampling.

00:07:51.015 --> 00:07:57.777
The shorter finalist is sixty six point three four miles and about seventy five and a half minutes.

00:07:57.777 --> 00:08:03.036
It has roughly eighty one percent known cell coverage, with six cells still unknown.

00:08:03.036 --> 00:08:09.047
The four route rounds used twenty six, fourteen, ten, and nine lookups: fifty nine in total.

00:08:09.047 --> 00:08:16.185
The separate map lookup brings the recorded session to sixty credits, or sixty cents at the session's planning rate.

00:08:16.185 --> 00:08:21.444
Those are the costs of this example, not a fixed price for every trip.

00:08:21.444 --> 00:08:24.825
Two finalists are an invitation to compare and inspect.

00:08:30.750 --> 00:08:35.583
We finish in Look around, with a known route cell selected for inspection.

00:08:35.583 --> 00:08:44.133
Street View can add visual context to a place on the route, but check the imagery date and remember that conditions can change.

00:08:44.133 --> 00:08:50.452
The property-value signal is not a safety verdict about the route or the people who live nearby.

00:08:50.452 --> 00:08:54.913
Combine the route comparison with your own inspection and current road information.

00:08:54.913 --> 00:08:59.002
This footage ends at the review controls, before the route choice.

00:08:59.002 --> 00:09:01.976
It does not show a completed driving recommendation.

00:09:01.976 --> 00:09:07.552
You can pause on these screens or use the chapter list to revisit a decision.

00:09:07.552 --> 00:09:13.500
The useful outcome is a better informed choice, with the remaining gaps in evidence still visible.
